/* KCNA mock bank 2: Container Orchestration (52), Cloud Native Application Delivery (30), Cloud Native Architecture (22). */
KS.registerMockBank('kcna', [

  /* ===================== ORCH: Networking (15) ===================== */
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which statement describes the Kubernetes network model?', options: ['Pods on different nodes must use NAT to reach each other', 'Every Pod gets its own IP address and can reach every other Pod without NAT', 'Containers in the same Pod each get a separate IP address', 'Pods can only communicate through Services'], answer: 1, explain: 'The model requires a <b>flat network</b>: each Pod has a unique IP and Pods talk directly, across nodes, without NAT. Containers inside one Pod share a single IP (they use localhost), and Services are an optional stable front end, not a requirement for Pod-to-Pod traffic.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'When a new Pod starts, which piece is invoked to create its network interface and assign its IP address?', options: ['kube-proxy', 'CoreDNS', 'The CNI plugin', 'The kube-scheduler'], answer: 2, explain: 'The container runtime calls the <b>CNI plugin</b> (Calico, Cilium, Flannel…) to wire the Pod into the network and allocate its IP. kube-proxy programs Service rules, CoreDNS answers name lookups and the scheduler only picks a node.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Your apps should reach an external managed database at <code>db.prod.example.com</code> through the in-cluster name <code>orders-db</code>, so the hostname can change later without touching the apps. Which Service type fits?', options: ['ClusterIP', 'NodePort', 'LoadBalancer', 'ExternalName'], answer: 3, explain: 'An <b>ExternalName</b> Service has no selector and no proxying: cluster DNS simply returns a CNAME to the external hostname. ClusterIP, NodePort and LoadBalancer all expose Pods inside the cluster rather than aliasing an outside name.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Service of type NodePort is created without specifying <code>nodePort</code>. From which range is the port allocated by default?', options: ['30000-32767', '1024-65535', '8000-8999', '80-443'], answer: 0, explain: 'The API server allocates NodePorts from <b>30000-32767</b> by default (configurable with <code>--service-node-port-range</code>). The port is opened on every node.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A StatefulSet needs each replica to be reachable by a stable DNS name such as <code>db-0.db.data.svc.cluster.local</code>. What kind of Service should it reference?', options: ['A LoadBalancer Service', 'A NodePort Service', 'An ExternalName Service', 'A headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>)'], answer: 3, explain: 'A <b>headless Service</b> has no virtual IP; DNS returns the individual Pod IPs and, together with a StatefulSet, creates per-Pod records like <code>db-0.db</code>. The other types give a single virtual address or an alias, not per-Pod names.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which API object does Kubernetes use to track the set of backend Pod IPs and ports for a Service, split into chunks so large Services scale well?', options: ['EndpointSlice', 'IngressClass', 'NetworkPolicy', 'ServiceAccount'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>EndpointSlices</b> (discovery.k8s.io) hold the ready backend addresses for a Service, by default up to 100 endpoints per slice, so an update touches one small object instead of one huge Endpoints list. The other objects are unrelated to backend tracking.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Pod in namespace <code>web</code> must call Service <code>payments</code> in namespace <code>billing</code>. Which DNS name works with the default cluster domain?', options: ['<code>billing.payments.svc.cluster.local</code>', '<code>payments.svc.billing.cluster.local</code>', '<code>payments.billing.svc.cluster.local</code>', '<code>payments.cluster.local</code>'], answer: 2, explain: 'Service records follow <b>&lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.&lt;cluster-domain&gt;</b>. Inside the same namespace the short name <code>payments</code> works; across namespaces you need at least <code>payments.billing</code>.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'You applied a valid Ingress resource but no traffic is routed and its ADDRESS column stays empty. What is the most likely cause?', options: ['The Service is of type ClusterIP', 'CoreDNS is not running', 'The Pods have no readiness probe', 'No Ingress controller is installed in the cluster'], answer: 3, explain: 'An Ingress is only a set of rules; an <b>Ingress controller</b> (for example ingress-nginx or a cloud controller) must watch it and actually route traffic. ClusterIP backends are exactly what Ingress normally targets, and missing probes or DNS would not leave the address empty.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which TWO of the following are resource kinds defined by the Kubernetes Gateway API? (Select 2)', options: ['GatewayClass', 'EndpointSlice', 'HTTPRoute', 'IngressClass'], answer: [0, 2], explain: 'The Gateway API is role-oriented: <b>GatewayClass</b> (infrastructure provider), <b>Gateway</b> (cluster operator) and routes such as <b>HTTPRoute</b> (application developer). IngressClass belongs to the older Ingress API, and EndpointSlice is core service discovery.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A team writes a NetworkPolicy that should block all traffic to the database Pods, but connections still succeed. The cluster uses a CNI plugin that only provides basic overlay networking. What explains this?', options: ['NetworkPolicies only apply to Services, not Pods', 'NetworkPolicies are enforced by the CNI plugin, and this plugin does not enforce them', 'kube-proxy must be restarted after creating a policy', 'NetworkPolicies only work in the default namespace'], answer: 1, explain: 'The API server stores NetworkPolicies, but enforcement is done by the <b>network plugin</b>. Plugins such as Calico or Cilium enforce them; a plain overlay such as Flannel does not, so the policy is silently ignored. Policies select Pods (not Services), work in any namespace and do not involve kube-proxy.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which NetworkPolicy creates a “default deny all ingress” for every Pod in its namespace?', options: ['<code>podSelector: {}</code> with <code>policyTypes: [Ingress]</code> and no ingress rules', '<code>podSelector: {}</code> with an ingress rule <code>from: [{}]</code>', 'A policy with <code>policyTypes: [Egress]</code> only', 'Deleting all Services in the namespace'], answer: 0, explain: 'An empty <code>podSelector</code> selects all Pods, and declaring <b>Ingress</b> with no allow rules isolates them completely. An ingress rule <code>from: [{}]</code> means “allow from everywhere”, an Egress-only policy leaves ingress open, and Services have nothing to do with policy.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which component programs rules on each node (for example with iptables or IPVS) so that traffic sent to a Service’s ClusterIP reaches one of its backend Pods?', options: ['kubelet', 'CoreDNS', 'kube-proxy', 'cloud-controller-manager'], answer: 2, explain: '<b>kube-proxy</b> watches Services and EndpointSlices and writes the forwarding rules on every node. Some CNI plugins (for example Cilium) can replace it with eBPF, but the job is the same. The kubelet manages Pods, CoreDNS resolves names, and the cloud controller creates external load balancers.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A security review asks for encrypted, mutually authenticated traffic between all microservices, plus per-request retries and traffic splitting, without changing application code. What should you add?', options: ['A NetworkPolicy per namespace', 'An Ingress controller', 'A headless Service for every app', 'A service mesh such as Istio or Linkerd'], answer: 3, explain: 'A <b>service mesh</b> puts a proxy next to each workload (often Envoy, or Linkerd’s own proxy) to provide automatic <b>mTLS</b>, retries and traffic shifting for east-west traffic. NetworkPolicies filter connections but do not encrypt; Ingress handles north-south traffic; headless Services only change DNS.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'How does a Service of type LoadBalancer relate to the other Service types on a typical cloud cluster?', options: ['It replaces kube-proxy on every node', 'It only works with an Ingress resource', 'It requires a headless Service to work', 'It builds on NodePort and ClusterIP: the external load balancer forwards to node ports that lead to the Pods'], answer: 3, explain: 'A LoadBalancer Service also gets a <b>ClusterIP</b> and (by default) a <b>NodePort</b>; the cloud-controller-manager provisions an external LB that sends traffic to those node ports. It does not replace kube-proxy and needs neither an Ingress nor a headless Service.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'You create a Service without a <code>selector</code>. What happens?', options: ['The API server rejects it', 'It automatically selects every Pod in the namespace', 'Kubernetes creates no EndpointSlices for it, so you must manage the endpoints yourself', 'It becomes an ExternalName Service'], answer: 2, explain: 'Without a selector the EndpointSlice controller has nothing to match, so <b>no endpoints are created automatically</b>. This is a valid pattern for pointing a Service at IPs outside the cluster by creating EndpointSlices manually. It is not rejected, does not select everything, and only becomes ExternalName if you set that type.' },

  /* ===================== ORCH: Security (15) ===================== */
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'In the “4Cs of cloud native security”, which layer is the innermost one?', options: ['Cloud', 'Cluster', 'Container', 'Code'], answer: 3, explain: 'The layers from outside in are <b>Cloud → Cluster → Container → Code</b>. Each layer builds on the security of the one around it, so strong code cannot compensate for a compromised cloud account.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'How are normal human users represented in Kubernetes?', options: ['As User objects created with <code>kubectl create user</code>', 'As ServiceAccounts in the kube-system namespace', 'They are not API objects; identities come from external sources such as client certificates or OIDC', 'As entries in a ConfigMap read by the API server'], answer: 2, explain: 'Kubernetes has <b>no User object</b>. The API server trusts identities proven by client certificates, OIDC tokens or other authenticators. ServiceAccounts are API objects, but they are meant for workloads, not people.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Pod running a custom controller must list Deployments through the Kubernetes API. Which identity should the Pod use?', options: ['A ServiceAccount bound to a suitable Role or ClusterRole', 'The cluster-admin client certificate copied into the image', 'The node’s kubelet credentials', 'An anonymous request'], answer: 0, explain: 'Workloads authenticate as a <b>ServiceAccount</b>, whose short-lived token is projected into the Pod; RBAC then grants exactly the verbs needed. Baking admin certificates into an image or borrowing kubelet credentials breaks least privilege, and anonymous access should be disabled or tightly limited.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A ServiceAccount needs read-only access to Pods in the <code>dev</code> namespace only. Which RBAC objects are the best fit?', options: ['ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding', 'Role and RoleBinding in <code>dev</code>', 'Role and ClusterRoleBinding', 'NetworkPolicy and RoleBinding'], answer: 1, explain: 'A namespaced <b>Role</b> plus a <b>RoleBinding</b> in <code>dev</code> grants permissions only there. A ClusterRoleBinding would grant cluster-wide, a ClusterRoleBinding cannot reference a Role, and NetworkPolicy controls traffic, not API access.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A RoleBinding in namespace <code>team-a</code> references the built-in ClusterRole <code>view</code>. What access does the subject get?', options: ['Read access to most objects in every namespace', 'Full admin access in <code>team-a</code>', 'No access, because a RoleBinding cannot reference a ClusterRole', 'Read access to most objects in <code>team-a</code> only'], answer: 3, explain: 'A RoleBinding <b>can</b> reference a ClusterRole; the permissions are then limited to the binding’s namespace. This lets you define a role once and reuse it per namespace. Cluster-wide access would need a ClusterRoleBinding, and <code>view</code> is read-only.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'An engineer wants an RBAC rule that says “everyone in group devs may do everything except delete Secrets”. Why is this not possible with RBAC alone?', options: ['RBAC only supports users, not groups', 'Secrets are not covered by RBAC', 'RBAC rules are purely additive; there are no deny rules', 'Delete is not a valid RBAC verb'], answer: 2, explain: 'RBAC is <b>allow-only</b>: permissions are the union of all bindings, and nothing can subtract from them. You must instead grant only the verbs you want. Groups are valid subjects, Secrets are a normal resource and <code>delete</code> is a standard verb.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which TWO are policy levels defined by the Pod Security Standards? (Select 2)', options: ['Baseline', 'Hardened', 'Restricted', 'Trusted'], answer: [0, 2], explain: 'The Pod Security Standards define three levels: <b>Privileged</b> (unrestricted), <b>Baseline</b> (blocks known privilege escalations) and <b>Restricted</b> (current hardening best practice). “Hardened” and “Trusted” are not levels.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Since PodSecurityPolicy was removed in v1.25, how do you make the built-in Pod Security Admission controller reject non-compliant Pods in a namespace?', options: ['Create a PodSecurityPolicy object in the namespace', 'Set the label <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted</code> on the namespace', 'Add a <code>securityContext</code> to the namespace spec', 'Install Falco in the namespace'], answer: 1, explain: 'Pod Security Admission is configured with <b>namespace labels</b>; the <code>enforce</code> mode rejects violating Pods, while <code>audit</code> and <code>warn</code> only record or warn. PodSecurityPolicy no longer exists, namespaces have no securityContext, and Falco detects runtime behaviour rather than blocking admission.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which <code>securityContext</code> setting makes the kubelet refuse to start a container whose image would run as UID 0?', options: ['<code>privileged: false</code>', '<code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code>', '<code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>', '<code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>'], answer: 2, explain: '<b>runAsNonRoot: true</b> makes the kubelet validate the effective user and refuse to run as root. The other settings are good hardening but address different risks: privileged mode, writes to the root filesystem and setuid-style escalation.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A developer says “our database password is safe because it is stored in a Kubernetes Secret”. What is the important caveat?', options: ['Secrets are only base64-encoded by default, so etcd encryption at rest and tight RBAC are needed', 'Secrets are encrypted with the node’s TPM by default', 'Secrets can only be read by the kube-system namespace', 'Secrets are automatically rotated every 24 hours'], answer: 0, explain: 'Secret values are <b>base64-encoded, not encrypted</b>, and stored as-is in etcd unless you configure encryption at rest (an EncryptionConfiguration on the API server, often with a KMS provider). Anyone with <code>get secrets</code> in the namespace can read them. There is no default TPM encryption or automatic rotation.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Your platform team wants to enforce rules such as “every Deployment must have an owner label” using policies written as Kubernetes YAML, without learning a new policy language. Which CNCF project fits best?', options: ['Falco', 'CoreDNS', 'Jaeger', 'Kyverno'], answer: 3, explain: '<b>Kyverno</b> is a policy engine that runs as an admission webhook and expresses policies as Kubernetes resources in YAML. OPA Gatekeeper does the same job but uses the Rego language. Falco detects runtime threats, Jaeger traces requests and CoreDNS serves DNS.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'You need an alert whenever someone opens an interactive shell inside a running production container. Which CNCF project is built for this kind of runtime threat detection?', options: ['Falco', 'Kyverno', 'Helm', 'Harbor'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>Falco</b> watches kernel system calls (via eBPF) and fires alerts on rules such as “shell spawned in a container”. Kyverno acts at admission time, before Pods run; Helm packages apps and Harbor is a registry.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which TWO practices directly strengthen software supply chain security for container images? (Select 2)', options: ['Signing images (for example with Sigstore cosign) and verifying signatures before deployment', 'Always deploying the <code>latest</code> tag', 'Generating an SBOM for each image and scanning it for known vulnerabilities', 'Running build containers as privileged'], answer: [0, 2], explain: '<b>Signing and verification</b> prove an image comes from your pipeline and was not altered; an <b>SBOM plus vulnerability scanning</b> (for example with Trivy) shows what is inside it. The <code>latest</code> tag is mutable and untraceable, and privileged builds widen the attack surface.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which type of admission webhook is allowed to change an incoming object, for example to inject a sidecar container?', options: ['Validating admission webhook', 'Authorization webhook', 'Authentication webhook', 'Mutating admission webhook'], answer: 3, explain: '<b>Mutating</b> admission webhooks run first and may modify the object (service meshes use this to inject sidecars). Validating webhooks run afterwards and may only accept or reject. Authentication and authorization webhooks decide who you are and what you may do, not what the object contains.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Pod never calls the Kubernetes API. Which setting reduces risk by not mounting an API token into it at all?', options: ['<code>hostNetwork: false</code>', '<code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>', '<code>imagePullPolicy: Always</code>', '<code>shareProcessNamespace: false</code>'], answer: 1, explain: 'Setting <b>automountServiceAccountToken: false</b> (on the Pod or its ServiceAccount) keeps the token out of the container, so a compromised app cannot use it against the API server. The other fields concern networking, image pulls and process visibility.' },

  /* ===================== ORCH: Storage (11) ===================== */
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Pod uses an <code>emptyDir</code> volume as scratch space. When is that data deleted?', options: ['When any container in the Pod restarts', 'When the node reboots, even if the Pod survives', 'Never; it is stored in etcd', 'When the Pod is removed from its node'], answer: 3, explain: 'An <b>emptyDir</b> lives as long as the Pod: it survives container restarts but is erased when the Pod is deleted or evicted. It is node-local disk (or memory), never etcd.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Why is a <code>hostPath</code> volume generally discouraged for application workloads?', options: ['It cannot be mounted read-only', 'It only works with StatefulSets', 'It ties the Pod to one node’s filesystem and can expose sensitive host files', 'It is deleted when the container restarts'], answer: 2, explain: '<b>hostPath</b> mounts a directory from the node, so data does not follow the Pod to other nodes and a bad path (for example the container runtime socket) can give a container control over the host. It can be read-only, works with any workload and survives restarts.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which statement correctly contrasts a PersistentVolume (PV) and a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)?', options: ['A PV is namespaced; a PVC is cluster-scoped', 'A PV is a piece of storage in the cluster; a PVC is a namespaced request for storage that binds to a PV', 'A PVC is created by the kubelet; a PV is created by the scheduler', 'A PV stores configuration; a PVC stores secrets'], answer: 1, explain: 'A <b>PV</b> is a cluster-scoped storage resource (pre-created or dynamically provisioned). A <b>PVC</b> is a namespaced claim by a user that binds one-to-one to a matching PV. Neither is created by the kubelet or the scheduler.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A developer creates a PVC that names a StorageClass, and a matching volume is created automatically in the cloud. Which feature made this happen?', options: ['Static provisioning', 'Volume snapshots', 'Dynamic provisioning through the StorageClass’s provisioner', 'A hostPath volume'], answer: 2, explain: 'With <b>dynamic provisioning</b>, the StorageClass names a provisioner (usually a CSI driver) that creates a new PV on demand for each PVC. Static provisioning means an admin pre-creates PVs; snapshots copy existing volumes; hostPath is node-local.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A volume must be mounted read-write by exactly one Pod in the whole cluster, even if other Pods are scheduled on the same node. Which access mode guarantees this?', options: ['ReadWriteOnce', 'ReadOnlyMany', 'ReadWriteMany', 'ReadWriteOncePod'], answer: 3, explain: '<b>ReadWriteOncePod</b> restricts the volume to a single Pod. The classic trap: <b>ReadWriteOnce</b> limits mounting to a single <i>node</i>, so several Pods on that node can still share it. ReadOnlyMany and ReadWriteMany allow many nodes.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A dynamically provisioned PVC is deleted. Its PV disappears and the cloud disk is destroyed. Which setting caused this, and what would have kept the data?', options: ['<code>reclaimPolicy: Delete</code>; setting <code>Retain</code> would have kept the volume', '<code>accessModes: ReadWriteOnce</code>; using ReadWriteMany would have kept it', '<code>volumeMode: Filesystem</code>; using Block would have kept it', '<code>storage: 10Gi</code>; a larger size would have kept it'], answer: 0, explain: 'The <b>reclaim policy</b> decides what happens to a PV after its claim is released. Dynamically provisioned volumes inherit the StorageClass’s policy, which defaults to <b>Delete</b>. <b>Retain</b> keeps the PV and data for manual recovery. Access modes, volume mode and size do not affect deletion.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'What is the main benefit of the Container Storage Interface (CSI)?', options: ['It encrypts all Secrets in etcd', 'It schedules Pods onto nodes with free disk', 'It replaces the need for PersistentVolumeClaims', 'It lets storage vendors ship drivers outside the Kubernetes codebase through a standard interface'], answer: 3, explain: '<b>CSI</b> is a standard API between orchestrators and storage systems, so vendors release drivers on their own schedule instead of adding in-tree code to Kubernetes. PVCs are still how apps request storage; CSI is how the storage is actually provided.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A StatefulSet with <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> is scaled from 3 to 1 replica. With default settings, what happens to the PVCs of the removed Pods?', options: ['They are deleted together with the Pods', 'They are moved to the remaining Pod', 'They are kept, so the data is reattached if the StatefulSet scales up again', 'They are converted into emptyDir volumes'], answer: 2, explain: 'By default StatefulSet PVCs are <b>retained</b> when Pods are removed, so <code>data-db-1</code> and <code>data-db-2</code> reattach to the same ordinals on scale-up. Automatic cleanup is opt-in via <code>persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy</code>. PVCs never move between Pods.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A new PVC stays in <code>Pending</code>. It does not set <code>storageClassName</code> and there are no pre-created PVs. What is the most likely cause?', options: ['The cluster has no default StorageClass', 'The Pod using it has no readiness probe', 'kube-proxy is not running', 'The namespace has a NetworkPolicy'], answer: 0, explain: 'A PVC without a class uses the <b>default StorageClass</b>; if none is marked default and no static PV matches, nothing can satisfy it and it stays Pending. Probes, kube-proxy and NetworkPolicies are unrelated to volume binding.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'In a multi-zone cluster, a StorageClass sets <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code>. Why?', options: ['To keep the volume from being deleted', 'To delay provisioning until a Pod is scheduled, so the volume is created in that Pod’s zone', 'To let many Pods write to the volume at once', 'To encrypt the volume before first use'], answer: 1, explain: 'With <b>WaitForFirstConsumer</b>, binding waits for the scheduler to place the Pod, then the volume is provisioned where that Pod can reach it. With <code>Immediate</code>, a disk might be created in a zone where the Pod cannot run. It has nothing to do with deletion, sharing or encryption.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'An application needs its <code>app.properties</code> file to appear under <code>/etc/app/</code> without rebuilding the image. Which approach is most appropriate?', options: ['Store the file in a PersistentVolume with ReadWriteMany', 'Copy it in with <code>kubectl cp</code> after every restart', 'Mount a ConfigMap as a volume at <code>/etc/app/</code>', 'Put the file in an emptyDir volume'], answer: 2, explain: 'A <b>ConfigMap volume</b> projects each key as a file and is the standard way to inject non-secret configuration files. A PV is overkill for config, <code>kubectl cp</code> is lost on restart, and an emptyDir starts empty.' },

  /* ===================== ORCH: Troubleshooting (11) ===================== */
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Pod shows <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> with 7 restarts. Which command is most useful to see why the last attempt failed?', options: ['<code>kubectl get nodes</code>', '<code>kubectl get svc</code>', '<code>kubectl top pod mypod</code>', '<code>kubectl logs mypod --previous</code>'], answer: 3, explain: 'In CrashLoopBackOff the current container may have just restarted, so <b><code>--previous</code></b> shows the output of the instance that crashed. Node lists, resource usage and Services rarely explain an application crash.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which TWO are common causes of a Pod stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>? (Select 2)', options: ['A typo in the image tag', 'The container exceeded its memory limit', 'A private registry with no valid <code>imagePullSecrets</code>', 'A failing liveness probe'], answer: [0, 2], explain: 'ImagePullBackOff means the kubelet <b>cannot pull the image</b>: a wrong name or tag, missing registry credentials or a network problem. Memory limits (OOMKilled) and liveness probes only matter after the image is pulled and the container runs.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: '<code>kubectl describe pod</code> shows <code>Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137</code>. What happened?', options: ['The image could not be pulled', 'The node ran out of disk', 'The readiness probe failed', 'The container used more memory than its limit and was killed'], answer: 3, explain: '<b>OOMKilled</b> means the kernel killed the process for exceeding its <b>memory limit</b>; exit code 137 is 128 + 9 (SIGKILL). Fix by raising the limit or reducing memory use. Pull, disk and probe problems show different reasons.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A new Pod stays <code>Pending</code> and its events say <code>0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu</code>. What is the most direct fix?', options: ['Lower the Pod’s CPU request or add node capacity', 'Raise the Pod’s CPU limit', 'Add a readiness probe', 'Change the restartPolicy to Never'], answer: 0, explain: 'The scheduler places Pods by <b>requests</b>; no node has enough unreserved CPU. Lower the request, free capacity or add nodes (for example with Cluster Autoscaler). Raising the limit does not help scheduling, and probes or restart policy only matter after scheduling.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A node shows <code>NotReady</code>. SSH works fine. What should you check first on that node?', options: ['The CoreDNS Deployment', 'The kubelet service status and logs (for example <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>)', 'The Ingress controller logs', 'The etcd member list'], answer: 1, explain: 'Node readiness is reported by the <b>kubelet</b>. If it is stopped, misconfigured, cannot reach the API server or the container runtime is down, the node goes NotReady. CoreDNS and Ingress are workloads, and etcd lives on the control plane.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Which command lists recent events in a namespace sorted by time, a quick way to spot scheduling, pull and probe failures?', options: ['<code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code>', '<code>kubectl logs --all-namespaces</code>', '<code>kubectl api-resources</code>', '<code>kubectl cluster-info</code>'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>Events</b> record what controllers and the kubelet did (FailedScheduling, BackOff, Unhealthy…). <code>kubectl describe</code> shows the events for one object. <code>kubectl logs</code> needs a Pod, api-resources lists types and cluster-info prints endpoints.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Service exists and its Pods are Running, but <code>kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web</code> shows no endpoints. What is the most likely cause?', options: ['CoreDNS is down', 'The Service type should be LoadBalancer', 'The Service selector does not match the Pod labels', 'The Pods have no resource limits'], answer: 2, explain: 'Endpoints come from the <b>selector</b>; a typo such as <code>app: web</code> vs <code>app: frontend</code> leaves the Service with no backends. DNS would still resolve the ClusterIP, the type does not affect endpoint selection, and limits are irrelevant.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'Pods can reach Services by IP but name lookups like <code>nslookup kubernetes.default</code> time out. Where should you look first?', options: ['The kube-scheduler logs', 'The HorizontalPodAutoscaler', 'The PersistentVolume list', 'The CoreDNS Pods in <code>kube-system</code> and the <code>kube-dns</code> Service'], answer: 3, explain: 'If IP traffic works but names fail, cluster DNS is the suspect: check that the <b>CoreDNS</b> Pods are Running and that the <code>kube-dns</code> Service has endpoints. The scheduler, storage and autoscaling do not take part in name resolution.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Pod is <code>Running</code> but shows <code>0/1</code> in the READY column, and it receives no traffic from its Service. What is the likely reason?', options: ['The liveness probe is failing and the container is being killed', 'The image is still being pulled', 'The Pod is not scheduled', 'The readiness probe is failing, so the Pod is removed from the Service’s endpoints'], answer: 3, explain: 'A failing <b>readiness probe</b> marks the container not ready and takes the Pod out of Service endpoints without restarting it. A failing liveness probe would cause restarts; pulling or unscheduled Pods would not show Running.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Pod shows status <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code>. What is a typical cause?', options: ['It references a ConfigMap or Secret (for example in <code>envFrom</code>) that does not exist', 'The node is out of CPU', 'The container image is too large', 'The Service has no endpoints'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>CreateContainerConfigError</b> means the kubelet cannot build the container configuration, most often because a referenced <b>ConfigMap or Secret</b> or key is missing. CPU shortage gives Pending, image problems give ErrImagePull, and Service endpoints do not affect container creation.' },
  { domain: 'orch', q: 'A Deployment runs a script that finishes its work and exits with code 0. The Pod keeps restarting and ends up in CrashLoopBackOff. Why?', options: ['Exit code 0 is treated as a failure by Kubernetes', 'Deployment Pods use <code>restartPolicy: Always</code>, so any exit triggers a restart; run-to-completion work belongs in a Job', 'The image has no CMD instruction', 'The readiness probe is missing'], answer: 1, explain: 'Pods managed by a Deployment must use <b>restartPolicy: Always</b>, so even a successful exit is restarted, with growing back-off. Use a <b>Job</b> (or CronJob) for tasks that should finish. Exit code 0 is success; CMD and probes are not the issue.' },

  /* ===================== DELIVERY: GitOps (8) ===================== */
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'Which TWO are among the four OpenGitOps principles? (Select 2)', options: ['Pulled automatically', 'Imperatively applied', 'Continuously reconciled', 'Manually approved'], answer: [0, 2], explain: 'The OpenGitOps principles are: <b>Declarative</b>, <b>Versioned and immutable</b>, <b>Pulled automatically</b> and <b>Continuously reconciled</b>. Imperative commands and manual approval steps are not part of the definition.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'Compared with a CI pipeline that runs <code>kubectl apply</code> against production (push model), what is a key security advantage of the pull-based GitOps model?', options: ['Images are built faster', 'Tests no longer need to run', 'The external CI system no longer needs credentials to the cluster, because an in-cluster agent pulls changes', 'Kubernetes RBAC is no longer required'], answer: 2, explain: 'In the <b>pull</b> model an agent such as Argo CD or Flux runs inside the cluster and fetches the desired state, so cluster credentials never leave it. Build speed and testing are unchanged, and RBAC still governs the agent itself.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'An engineer runs <code>kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=10</code> on a cluster managed by Argo CD with automated sync and self-heal enabled. Git says 3 replicas. What happens?', options: ['Argo CD detects the drift and restores 3 replicas', 'Argo CD commits 10 replicas back to Git', 'The Deployment is deleted', 'The change is permanent until someone edits Git'], answer: 0, explain: 'With <b>self-heal</b>, the agent continuously compares live state with Git and reverts manual changes, so the Deployment returns to <b>3</b>. GitOps agents do not write live changes back to Git. To change replicas, change Git.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'In Argo CD, which custom resource defines the Git source (repo, path, revision) and the destination cluster and namespace for a set of manifests?', options: ['Kustomization', 'HelmRelease', 'GitRepository', 'Application'], answer: 3, explain: 'Argo CD’s core CRD is the <b>Application</b>, which links a source to a destination (ApplicationSet generates many of them). <code>Kustomization</code>, <code>HelmRelease</code> and <code>GitRepository</code> are Flux resources.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'The Argo CD UI shows an application as <b>OutOfSync</b> but <b>Healthy</b>. What does that mean?', options: ['The Pods are crashing', 'The application was deleted', 'Git cannot be reached', 'The live resources differ from what is in Git, but they are currently working'], answer: 3, explain: 'Argo CD tracks two separate things: <b>sync status</b> (does live state match Git?) and <b>health</b> (are the resources working?). OutOfSync + Healthy means there is a difference to apply, while the current resources are running fine. Crashing Pods would show Degraded.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'In Flux, which controller fetches manifests from Git repositories, Helm repositories or OCI registries and makes them available as artifacts to the other controllers?', options: ['source-controller', 'kustomize-controller', 'notification-controller', 'kube-controller-manager'], answer: 0, explain: 'Flux is built from the GitOps Toolkit: <b>source-controller</b> fetches and packages sources; <b>kustomize-controller</b> and <b>helm-controller</b> apply them; <b>notification-controller</b> handles alerts and webhooks. kube-controller-manager is a Kubernetes control plane component, not part of Flux.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'What is the CNCF maturity level of both Argo and Flux?', options: ['Sandbox', 'Incubating', 'Graduated', 'Archived'], answer: 2, explain: 'Both <b>Argo</b> and <b>Flux</b> are <b>graduated</b> CNCF projects, the highest maturity level, alongside projects such as Kubernetes, Prometheus and Helm.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'A release managed through GitOps introduced a bug. What is the GitOps-native way to roll back?', options: ['Run <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> on the cluster', 'Revert the commit in Git and let the agent reconcile', 'Delete the namespace and recreate it', 'SSH into the nodes and restart the containers'], answer: 1, explain: 'Git is the <b>single source of truth</b>, so rollback is a <b>git revert</b>; the agent then applies the previous state, with full audit history. A manual <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> would be seen as drift and possibly undone by self-heal.' },

  /* ===================== DELIVERY: CI/CD (2) ===================== */
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'What distinguishes <b>continuous deployment</b> from <b>continuous delivery</b>?', options: ['Continuous deployment pushes every change that passes the pipeline to production automatically; continuous delivery keeps it releasable but needs a manual decision to release', 'Continuous delivery skips automated tests', 'Continuous deployment only builds images and never deploys them', 'They are two names for the same thing'], answer: 0, explain: 'Both keep the main branch always releasable. In <b>continuous delivery</b> a human decides when to release; in <b>continuous deployment</b> every green build goes to production automatically. Neither skips tests.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'In a typical CI + GitOps setup, which step belongs in the CI pipeline rather than in the GitOps agent?', options: ['Reconciling the cluster to match Git', 'Detecting drift in live resources', 'Building, testing and pushing the container image, then updating the image tag in the config repo', 'Pruning resources deleted from Git'], answer: 2, explain: '<b>CI</b> produces artifacts: it builds, tests and pushes images, then updates the desired state (directly or through an image-automation tool). The <b>GitOps agent</b> handles deployment: reconciling, drift detection and pruning.' },

  /* ===================== DELIVERY: Helm & Kustomize (5) ===================== */
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'In Helm terminology, what is a <b>release</b>?', options: ['A packaged chart stored in a repository', 'A Git tag created by Helm', 'The values.yaml file', 'An installed instance of a chart in a cluster, with its own name and revision history'], answer: 3, explain: 'A <b>chart</b> is the package, a <b>repository</b> stores charts, and a <b>release</b> is one installation of a chart (you can install the same chart several times under different release names). Each upgrade creates a new revision.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'After <code>helm upgrade shop ./chart</code> breaks the app, which command returns the release <code>shop</code> to its previous revision?', options: ['<code>helm rollback shop</code>', '<code>helm uninstall shop</code>', '<code>kubectl rollout undo helm/shop</code>', '<code>helm repo update</code>'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>helm rollback &lt;release&gt; [revision]</b> restores an earlier revision (the previous one if none is given). <code>helm history shop</code> lists revisions. Uninstall removes the release, and <code>helm repo update</code> only refreshes chart indexes.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'You want to install a chart with <code>replicaCount</code> set to 5 for production without editing the chart. Which option is correct?', options: ['Edit the chart’s templates directly', 'Pass <code>--set replicaCount=5</code> or a values file with <code>-f prod-values.yaml</code>', 'Use <code>kubectl scale</code> before installing', 'Rename the chart to prod'], answer: 1, explain: 'Charts are parameterised through <b>values</b>: defaults live in <code>values.yaml</code> and are overridden with <code>-f</code> files or <code>--set</code>. Editing templates forks the chart, and scaling before install has nothing to scale.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'What major architectural change did Helm 3 introduce compared with Helm 2?', options: ['It added a server-side component called Tiller', 'It switched charts from YAML to JSON', 'It removed Tiller, so Helm talks to the API server directly using the user’s own credentials and RBAC', 'It stopped supporting chart repositories'], answer: 2, explain: 'Helm 2 relied on the in-cluster <b>Tiller</b> component, often with broad permissions. Helm 3 <b>removed Tiller</b>: the client works directly with the API server under the caller’s RBAC. Charts are still YAML templates, and repositories (plus OCI registries) are supported.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'How does Kustomize customise manifests for different environments?', options: ['With Go templates and a values file', 'By running a server in the cluster that rewrites objects', 'By converting YAML into Helm charts', 'With a base plus overlays that patch plain YAML, without templating'], answer: 3, explain: 'Kustomize is <b>template-free</b>: a <b>base</b> holds plain manifests and <b>overlays</b> (dev, prod) apply patches, name prefixes, labels and image changes, all listed in <code>kustomization.yaml</code>. Go templates and values files are Helm’s approach.' },

  /* ===================== DELIVERY: Deployment strategies & progressive delivery (6) ===================== */
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'A Deployment uses <code>RollingUpdate</code> with <code>maxSurge: 1</code> and <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> and has 4 replicas. What happens during an update?', options: ['All 4 Pods are stopped, then 4 new Pods start', 'Traffic is split 50/50 between versions by a service mesh', 'Two Pods are replaced at a time with possible downtime', 'At most 5 Pods exist at once and the number of available Pods never drops below 4'], answer: 3, explain: '<b>maxSurge: 1</b> allows one extra Pod above the desired 4; <b>maxUnavailable: 0</b> means no old Pod is removed until a new one is ready. This gives a zero-downtime, one-at-a-time rollout. Stopping all first is Recreate; traffic splitting needs a mesh or rollout tool.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'A new release changes the database schema so old and new versions must never run at the same time. Brief downtime is acceptable. Which strategy fits?', options: ['Recreate', 'Canary', 'RollingUpdate with maxSurge 25%', 'Blue/green with both versions writing to the database'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>Recreate</b> stops all old Pods before starting new ones, so versions never overlap, at the cost of downtime. Rolling updates and canaries deliberately run both versions together, and blue/green with shared writes reintroduces the conflict.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'Which TWO strategies are built into the Kubernetes Deployment object via <code>spec.strategy.type</code>? (Select 2)', options: ['Canary', 'RollingUpdate', 'Blue/green', 'Recreate'], answer: [1, 3], explain: 'A Deployment supports only <b>RollingUpdate</b> (the default) and <b>Recreate</b>. Canary and blue/green are built from extra objects (two Deployments plus Service or Ingress changes) or with tools such as Argo Rollouts or Flagger.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'A team runs two full environments, switches all traffic from v1 to v2 at once by changing a Service selector, and can switch back instantly. Which strategy is this?', options: ['Canary', 'Rolling update', 'Blue/green', 'Recreate'], answer: 2, explain: '<b>Blue/green</b> keeps two complete environments and flips traffic in one step, giving instant rollback at the cost of double resources. A canary shifts a small share of traffic gradually; a rolling update replaces Pods in place.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'You want to send 5% of live traffic to a new version, watch error rates and latency, and widen or abort automatically. What is this called?', options: ['A canary release driven by progressive delivery', 'A Recreate deployment', 'A blue/green switch', 'A StatefulSet ordered update'], answer: 0, explain: 'Gradually shifting a small share of traffic is a <b>canary</b>; automating promotion or rollback from metrics is <b>progressive delivery</b>. Recreate and blue/green move all traffic at once, and StatefulSet ordering is about Pod identity, not traffic.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'Which TWO tools are commonly used to automate canary analysis and progressive delivery on Kubernetes? (Select 2)', options: ['Kustomize', 'Argo Rollouts', 'Flagger', 'CoreDNS'], answer: [1, 2], explain: '<b>Argo Rollouts</b> (part of the Argo project) and <b>Flagger</b> (part of the Flux project) shift traffic in steps and query metrics to promote or roll back. Kustomize customises YAML and CoreDNS serves cluster DNS.' },

  /* ===================== DELIVERY: Operators (2) ===================== */
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'A vendor ships a PostgreSQL operator. What does an operator consist of?', options: ['A Helm chart with extra values', 'A custom resource definition plus a controller that encodes operational knowledge such as backups, failover and upgrades', 'A DaemonSet that runs on every node', 'A mutating webhook that adds sidecars'], answer: 1, explain: 'The <b>operator pattern</b> = <b>CRD</b> (a new API such as <code>PostgresCluster</code>) + a <b>custom controller</b> that reconciles it, automating what a human operator would do. It may be installed with Helm, but a chart alone does not reconcile anything.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'You install a CRD named <code>Backup</code> and create several Backup objects, but no backups are ever taken. Why?', options: ['CRDs must be created in kube-system', 'Custom resources require a ClusterIP Service', 'A CRD only adds a new API type; without a controller watching it, nothing acts on the objects', 'The API server deletes custom resources after one minute'], answer: 2, explain: 'A <b>CRD</b> just extends the API so objects can be stored and read. Behaviour comes from a <b>controller</b> (the operator) that watches those objects and acts. CRDs are cluster-scoped definitions and need no Service.' },

  /* ===================== DELIVERY: Debugging (7) ===================== */
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'A Pod has two containers, <code>app</code> and <code>proxy</code>. Which command streams the logs of the proxy only?', options: ['<code>kubectl logs mypod -f</code>', '<code>kubectl logs mypod -c proxy -f</code>', '<code>kubectl describe pod mypod proxy</code>', '<code>kubectl exec mypod -- logs proxy</code>'], answer: 1, explain: '<b><code>-c</code></b> picks the container and <b><code>-f</code></b> follows the stream. Without <code>-c</code> on a multi-container Pod, kubectl uses the default container (or asks you to choose). describe shows events, not logs.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'What does <code>kubectl exec -it web-7d9f -- sh</code> do?', options: ['Starts a new Pod named sh', 'Opens an interactive shell inside the running container of Pod web-7d9f', 'Restarts the Pod with a shell entrypoint', 'Copies a shell binary into the image'], answer: 1, explain: '<b>kubectl exec</b> runs a command in an existing container; <code>-it</code> attaches an interactive terminal. It fails if the image has no shell (for example distroless), which is where <code>kubectl debug</code> helps.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'You need to test a ClusterIP Service <code>api</code> (port 80) from your laptop without exposing it publicly. Which command fits?', options: ['<code>kubectl expose svc/api --type=LoadBalancer</code>', '<code>kubectl proxy svc/api</code>', '<code>kubectl port-forward svc/api 8080:80</code>', '<code>kubectl edit svc/api</code>'], answer: 2, explain: '<b>port-forward</b> tunnels local port 8080 through the API server to the Service (in practice one of its Pods) on port 80, with no change to the cluster. Exposing a LoadBalancer makes it public, and editing the Service changes shared config.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'A Pod runs a distroless image with no shell. Which approach lets you inspect it while it keeps running?', options: ['<code>kubectl debug -it mypod --image=busybox --target=app</code>, which adds an ephemeral container', '<code>kubectl exec -it mypod -- bash</code>', 'Rebuild the image with a shell and redeploy', '<code>kubectl attach mypod --shell</code>'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>kubectl debug</b> injects an <b>ephemeral container</b> with your tools into the running Pod; <code>--target</code> shares the process namespace of the app container. exec needs a shell in the image, rebuilding changes what you are debugging, and attach has no shell option.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'After a bad rollout, which command returns Deployment <code>web</code> to revision 2?', options: ['<code>kubectl rollout status deployment/web --revision=2</code>', '<code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/web --to-revision=2</code>', '<code>kubectl rollout restart deployment/web 2</code>', '<code>kubectl apply deployment/web --revision=2</code>'], answer: 1, explain: '<b>rollout undo</b> switches back to an earlier ReplicaSet; <code>--to-revision</code> picks which (see <code>kubectl rollout history</code>). <code>rollout status</code> only watches progress, and <code>rollout restart</code> re-creates Pods with the same spec.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: 'Before applying an edited manifest to production, you want to see exactly what would change in the live objects. Which command shows this?', options: ['<code>kubectl get -f app.yaml</code>', '<code>kubectl describe -f app.yaml</code>', '<code>kubectl diff -f app.yaml</code>', '<code>kubectl explain app.yaml</code>'], answer: 2, explain: '<b>kubectl diff</b> sends the manifest to the server as a dry run and prints a diff against live state. get and describe show current state only, and explain documents API fields.' },
  { domain: 'delivery', q: '<code>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml</code> fails with <code>strict decoding error: unknown field "spec.replica"</code>. What is wrong?', options: ['The cluster has no nodes', 'The image tag does not exist', 'The user lacks RBAC permission to create Deployments', 'The manifest has a misspelled field; it should be <code>replicas</code>'], answer: 3, explain: 'The API server validates fields against the schema and rejects <b>unknown fields</b>, here a typo for <code>replicas</code>. Missing nodes or images only appear later (Pending, ImagePullBackOff), and an RBAC problem returns <code>Forbidden</code>.' },

  /* ===================== ARCH: Observability (7) ===================== */
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'How does Prometheus normally collect metrics from applications?', options: ['Applications push metrics to Prometheus over gRPC', 'Prometheus scrapes (pulls) an HTTP endpoint such as <code>/metrics</code> at regular intervals', 'Prometheus reads metrics from etcd', 'Prometheus tails container log files'], answer: 1, explain: 'Prometheus is <b>pull-based</b>: it discovers targets (for example via Kubernetes service discovery) and scrapes their <code>/metrics</code> endpoint. Short-lived jobs can use the Pushgateway, but pulling is the default model. It does not read etcd or logs.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'Which Prometheus metric type fits <b>current memory usage</b> of a container, a value that can rise and fall?', options: ['Counter', 'Histogram', 'Gauge', 'Summary'], answer: 2, explain: 'A <b>gauge</b> is a single value that can go up and down (memory, queue length, temperature). A <b>counter</b> only increases (requests served) and is used with <code>rate()</code>. Histograms and summaries describe distributions such as latency.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'What does OpenTelemetry provide?', options: ['A time-series database for metrics', 'A vendor-neutral set of APIs, SDKs and a Collector for producing and exporting traces, metrics and logs', 'A log shipper that only supports Elasticsearch', 'A dashboard tool for visualising Prometheus data'], answer: 1, explain: '<b>OpenTelemetry</b> standardises how telemetry is <b>generated and exported</b>, so you can switch backends without re-instrumenting. It is not a storage backend (Prometheus, Jaeger) or a dashboard tool (Grafana). Do not confuse it with OpenMetrics, which is a metrics exposition format.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'A request passes through five microservices and is slow. Which tool shows the request as a trace of spans so you can see which hop added the delay?', options: ['Jaeger', 'Fluent Bit', 'Helm', 'etcd'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>Jaeger</b> is a CNCF distributed tracing system: each service records <b>spans</b> that are linked into one trace. Fluent Bit ships logs, Helm installs apps and etcd stores cluster state.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'You need a lightweight agent on every node to collect container logs and forward them to a central backend. Which tool and workload type is a common choice?', options: ['Jaeger as a Deployment', 'Prometheus as a StatefulSet', 'Fluent Bit as a DaemonSet', 'CoreDNS as a sidecar'], answer: 2, explain: '<b>Fluent Bit</b> (the lightweight sibling of <b>Fluentd</b>) runs as a <b>DaemonSet</b> so each node’s log files are read locally and forwarded. Jaeger handles traces, Prometheus metrics, and CoreDNS name resolution.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'Which statement correctly relates SLI, SLO and SLA?', options: ['The SLA is the measurement, the SLI the internal goal and the SLO the contract', 'The SLI is a measured indicator (for example request success rate), the SLO is the internal target for it, and the SLA is the external agreement with consequences if missed', 'They are three names for the same uptime number', 'The SLO is only used for billing, the SLI only for alerts'], answer: 1, explain: '<b>SLI</b> = what you measure, <b>SLO</b> = the target you aim for, <b>SLA</b> = the promise to customers, usually with penalties. SLOs are normally stricter than SLAs so you get warned before breaking the contract.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'A service has an availability SLO of 99.9% over 30 days. Roughly how much total downtime does its error budget allow in that window?', options: ['About 4.3 minutes', 'About 7.2 hours', 'About 43 minutes', 'About 4.3 hours'], answer: 2, explain: '30 days = 43,200 minutes; 0.1% of that is about <b>43 minutes</b>. The <b>error budget</b> is the allowed unreliability: while it lasts, teams can ship faster; once it is spent, they focus on reliability.' },

  /* ===================== ARCH: Ecosystem & Principles (8) ===================== */
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'Which TWO are required for a HorizontalPodAutoscaler to scale a Deployment on CPU utilisation? (Select 2)', options: ['A metrics source such as metrics-server', 'A service mesh', 'CPU requests set on the containers', 'A PersistentVolumeClaim'], answer: [0, 2], explain: 'The HPA reads CPU usage from the <b>resource metrics API</b> (usually metrics-server) and computes utilisation as a percentage of the <b>CPU request</b>. Without requests there is no denominator. A mesh and storage are irrelevant.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'Several Pods are Pending because no node has enough free CPU, even though the HPA wants more replicas. Which component adds nodes to the cluster?', options: ['Vertical Pod Autoscaler', 'Cluster Autoscaler', 'kube-scheduler', 'HorizontalPodAutoscaler'], answer: 1, explain: 'The <b>Cluster Autoscaler</b> watches for Pods that cannot be scheduled and grows the node group (and shrinks it when nodes are underused). HPA changes replica counts, VPA changes Pod requests, and the scheduler places Pods but cannot create nodes.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'A worker should scale with the length of a message queue, including down to zero replicas when the queue is empty. Which CNCF project is designed for this?', options: ['KEDA', 'Vertical Pod Autoscaler', 'Linkerd', 'Harbor'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>KEDA</b> (Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling) uses scalers for queues, streams and many other event sources and can scale to and from <b>zero</b>, working alongside the HPA. VPA changes resource requests, Linkerd is a mesh and Harbor a registry.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'Which specification defines a common format for describing event data, so events can move between services and platforms such as Knative Eventing?', options: ['OpenMetrics', 'CloudEvents', 'Container Network Interface', 'OCI Distribution Spec'], answer: 1, explain: '<b>CloudEvents</b> (a CNCF project) defines common event attributes such as id, source and type. Knative (Serving for scale-to-zero request-driven workloads, Eventing for event routing) uses it. OpenMetrics is a metrics format, CNI is networking, and OCI Distribution is about registries.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'A cluster replaces containerd with CRI-O and Flannel with Cilium, without any change to Kubernetes itself. Which TWO open interfaces make these swaps possible? (Select 2)', options: ['Container Storage Interface (CSI)', 'Container Runtime Interface (CRI)', 'OCI Distribution Specification', 'Container Network Interface (CNI)'], answer: [1, 3], explain: 'The kubelet talks to any compliant runtime through the <b>CRI</b>, and Pod networking is delegated to any plugin that implements the <b>CNI</b>. CSI covers storage drivers, which were not changed, and the OCI Distribution Spec defines how registries serve images.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'According to the Twelve-Factor App methodology, where should configuration such as database URLs live?', options: ['Hard-coded in the source code', 'In the environment, separate from the code (in Kubernetes, typically ConfigMaps, Secrets and env vars)', 'In the container image layers', 'In a shared file on one specific server'], answer: 1, explain: 'Factor III, <b>Config</b>, says store config in the <b>environment</b> so the same build runs in every stage. Baking config into code or images forces a rebuild per environment, and a file on one server breaks disposability.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'Which TWO are graduated CNCF projects? (Select 2)', options: ['Prometheus', 'Jenkins', 'Envoy', 'Terraform'], answer: [0, 2], explain: '<b>Prometheus</b> (monitoring) and <b>Envoy</b> (edge and service proxy) are both graduated CNCF projects. Jenkins belongs to the Continuous Delivery Foundation, and Terraform is a HashiCorp product, not a CNCF project.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'Which CNCF project is a cloud native container registry that adds features such as RBAC, vulnerability scanning and image replication?', options: ['Rook', 'Vitess', 'Harbor', 'containerd'], answer: 2, explain: '<b>Harbor</b> is the CNCF registry project. <b>Rook</b> orchestrates storage (notably Ceph) on Kubernetes, <b>Vitess</b> scales MySQL horizontally, and <b>containerd</b> is a container runtime.' },

  /* ===================== ARCH: Community & Collaboration (7) ===================== */
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'Which CNCF body votes to accept new projects and to move them between maturity levels?', options: ['The Technical Oversight Committee (TOC)', 'The Kubernetes Steering Committee', 'SIG Release', 'The Governing Board’s marketing committee'], answer: 0, explain: 'The <b>TOC</b> is the CNCF’s technical leadership: it defines the technical vision, accepts projects and approves moves from sandbox to incubating to graduated. The Kubernetes Steering Committee governs only the Kubernetes project, and SIG Release manages Kubernetes releases.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'What is the correct order of CNCF project maturity levels, from earliest to most mature?', options: ['Incubating → Sandbox → Graduated', 'Sandbox → Graduated → Incubating', 'Alpha → Beta → Stable', 'Sandbox → Incubating → Graduated'], answer: 3, explain: 'Projects enter as <b>Sandbox</b> (early, experimental), move to <b>Incubating</b> (growing adoption) and reach <b>Graduated</b> (proven, widely adopted, with a security audit and governance requirements). Alpha/beta/stable describes Kubernetes API and feature stages, not CNCF project maturity.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'Within the CNCF, which groups provide subject-area expertise (for example security or observability) and support the TOC in reviewing projects?', options: ['Kubernetes Enhancement Proposals', 'Technical Advisory Groups (TAGs)', 'Ingress controllers', 'Release teams'], answer: 1, explain: 'CNCF <b>TAGs</b> (Technical Advisory Groups) gather domain experts who write guidance, review projects and advise the TOC. KEPs are Kubernetes design documents, and release teams ship Kubernetes versions.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'You want to contribute to how Kubernetes handles Services and DNS. Which community group owns that area of the codebase?', options: ['A Kubernetes SIG, in this case SIG Network', 'The CNCF TOC', 'A CNCF Sandbox project', 'The OCI technical board'], answer: 0, explain: 'Kubernetes development is organised into <b>Special Interest Groups</b> (SIGs) that own specific areas: SIG Network, SIG Storage, SIG Node, SIG Auth and others. The TOC oversees CNCF projects in general, and OCI defines container specs.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'A contributor wants to add a significant new feature to Kubernetes. What process document must be written and approved by the owning SIG?', options: ['A CNCF due-diligence report', 'A Helm chart', 'A Kubernetes Enhancement Proposal (KEP)', 'A Pull Request template'], answer: 2, explain: 'Significant changes go through a <b>KEP</b>, which records motivation, design, graduation criteria (alpha → beta → GA) and the production-readiness review, under a sponsoring SIG. Due-diligence reports are for CNCF project moves, not Kubernetes features.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'What is KubeCon + CloudNativeCon?', options: ['The CNCF’s flagship conference, bringing together users and maintainers of Kubernetes and other cloud native projects', 'The name of the Kubernetes release schedule', 'A certification exam for cluster administrators', 'A CNCF graduated project for multi-cluster management'], answer: 0, explain: '<b>KubeCon + CloudNativeCon</b> is the CNCF’s main conference, held in several regions each year, with project updates and maintainer sessions. It is not a release cycle, exam or project.' },
  { domain: 'arch', q: 'A team builds an internal developer platform with self-service templates, golden paths and shared CI/CD so product teams can ship without learning every infrastructure detail. Which role does this describe?', options: ['Site Reliability Engineer focused on SLOs and incident response', 'Platform engineer', 'Database administrator', 'Security auditor'], answer: 1, explain: '<b>Platform engineering</b> treats the platform as a product for internal developers, reducing their cognitive load. <b>SREs</b> apply software engineering to operations, with a focus on reliability, SLOs, error budgets and incidents. The roles overlap but have different goals.' },
]);
